# Code ownership for TrustForge Contracts.
#
# GitHub requires review from the listed owner(s) on any PR touching these
# paths, once the repo's branch protection rule enables "Require review from
# Code Owners" for `main` (a GitHub repo setting, not something this file
# controls on its own -- see the note at the bottom).
#
# Only @hartz0 is listed today, per QUALITY_UPGRADE_ROADMAP.md Phase 6. This
# is a starting point, not a claim that bus-factor risk is solved: this repo
# has had 197 contributors historically (see git shortlog), and a single
# named owner is a first step toward "someone specific is accountable for
# contract-logic correctness," not a finished governance model. Expand this
# list as more maintainers are formally designated.

# Contract logic -- the highest-trust surface in this repo. Anyone can still
# open a PR; this just means it can't merge without a named owner's review.
/contracts/*/src/ @hartz0

# CI and size/security gates that decide what contract logic is even allowed
# to merge -- changing these has the same blast radius as changing the logic
# they gate.
/.github/workflows/ @hartz0
/scripts/ @hartz0

# Everything else (docs/**, README.md, top-level *.md files, issue/PR
# templates) is intentionally NOT owned here. Per CONTRIBUTING.md, doc and
# typo fixes should stay easy to merge without requiring a named reviewer --
# that's a deliberate choice, not an oversight.

# --- To make this enforced, not just advisory ---
# GitHub only blocks merges on CODEOWNERS review if the repo has a branch
# protection rule on `main` with "Require review from Code Owners" enabled.
# This file alone does not turn that on -- that's a GitHub repo setting
# (Settings > Branches > Branch protection rules) outside what a commit to
# this repository can configure.
